Miriam looked into the lens. “The ritual ended the session. The group stopped provoking. I perceive less pressure. Those statements are supportable. I regard it as success within my belief. The recording proves less.”

He lowered the camera slightly.

Diane opened the facilities case beneath Abby’s lens. The plastic evidence sleeve around the meter had softened from the warmth inside the case. The device housing appeared dry. She stated the time, cut the seal, and placed the meter on the shop towel.

“The replacement batteries remain installed,” she said. “The device has rested in a closed case near room temperature for forty-seven minutes. No electrical test has followed the earlier contact cleaning.”

Evan stepped closer. “Turn it on.”

Diane angled the meter so both cameras could see the display and switch.

“One press.”

She pressed the button.

Nothing appeared.

The assistant’s arms tightened around her ribs.

Diane kept her thumb away from the switch. “No power on first press.”

Evan exhaled through his nose.

“Wait,” Miriam said.

Diane looked at her. “For what?”

“For you to complete the test.”

Diane removed the battery cover. The cells sat in place. She pressed each one against its spring with a gloved fingertip, then released it. The dented original batteries remained sealed elsewhere; these were the labeled replacements. She showed the contacts to Abby’s camera. The gray residue had faded after cleaning, though a dark line remained along one edge of the positive plate.

“Possible intermittent contact,” Diane said.

She replaced the cover and set the meter flat on the towel.

“One press after reseating.”

The display illuminated. Every segment appeared, cleared, and settled at zero. The green indicator held steady.

The assistant covered her mouth.

Evan raised his camera. “It came back after the cleansing.”

“It came back after battery pressure and reseating,” Diane said.

“The timing still matters.”

“The timing belongs in the record. So does the handling.”

Miriam stepped beside the assistant. “Relief can follow the timing without proving the cause.”

The assistant nodded, though her eyes remained on the green light.

Abby stated the device state, handling sequence, and elapsed time. Diane left the meter running for sixty seconds. The reading moved between zero and one-tenth. The indicator stayed green.

Evan filmed the display, then Miriam, then the corridor behind them. “We came here to clear the school. The basement is quiet, the pressure is gone, and the equipment works.”

“The flashlight remains untested,” Diane said.

He looked toward the sealed sleeve.

“The door event remains unresolved. The audio remains disputed. The auditorium has received no final walk-through.”

“The basement is clear,” Miriam said. “That is my present impression.”

Diane closed the meter in a fresh evidence sleeve while it remained powered. “The basement session is complete. That wording enters my report.”

Evan gave a short laugh without amusement. “You can turn anything into paperwork.”

Diane sealed the sleeve. “I write down what your edit may leave out.”

A sound struck above them.

One low piano note traveled through the ceiling and the open stairwell. The tone held for several seconds, uneven and metallic, as though a heavy key on the auditorium’s upright piano had fallen and risen slowly.

Everyone looked toward the stairs.

The note stopped.

A second sound followed: wood scraping across the stage floor.

Miriam turned before Evan raised his camera.

“The basement may be clear,” she said.

Diane picked up the facilities case.

“Abby first,” she said. “Continuous recording to the auditorium.”

The assistant retrieved the blanket but left it folded over one arm.

Evan reached the stairwell door ahead of the others. Diane saw his hand close around the lever.

“Wait for the camera,” she said.

He held still.

Above them, somewhere beyond the auditorium doors, a seat snapped upright.

## Scene 8 — The Auditorium Search

Abby reached the stairwell landing before Evan touched the lever.

She filmed his hand, the lever, and the landing clock. Diane stated the time. Evan opened the door after Abby moved to the upper step, and the group climbed behind her in the same order they had left the basement.

The corridor outside the auditorium held cooler air. A vent above the trophy case ran at low speed. Salt had dried in gray fans across the tile from the side entrance. Beyond the double doors, aisle lamps divided the auditorium into descending strips of red upholstery and black floor. A single work light burned above the stage.

Diane stopped the group at the double doors.

“Positions first.”

Abby entered alone and held the camera on the center aisle. Empty red seats descended toward the stage in three sections. Several seats near the rear remained folded down. One near the front stood upright beside a lowered neighbor. The piano stood at stage left beneath a canvas cover. A wheeled scenery flat leaned near the opposite wing.

No person appeared in the aisles Abby’s camera covered.

Abby panned across the balcony rail, the lighting booth window, both exits, and the stage. She returned to the doors.

“Clear from this angle.”

Diane entered with the facilities case. Evan followed, then Miriam and her assistant. Their shoes pressed small wet marks into the carpet runner.

The assistant looked toward the front rows. “That seat moved.”

“The return spring raised it,” Diane said. “Question is what lowered it.”

She walked down the aisle without touching the seat. A knitted hat lay beneath the row. One edge of its pompom rested against the seat pan’s lower frame.

Diane crouched and aimed her flashlight beneath the cushion. “Possible obstruction.”

Evan filmed over her shoulder. “A hat cannot press a theater seat down.”

“The hat may show recent use. Someone may have left the seat lowered.”

“Who?”

Diane pointed toward the lobby. “Drama rehearsal ended at four. The auditorium received no sweep afterward.”

Abby marked the statement.

The piano note remained harder to place. Diane climbed onto the stage and pulled the canvas cover back far enough to expose the keyboard. The lowest white keys carried a thin line of dust. One key near the bass end sat slightly below its neighbors.

She pressed it.

The same uneven metallic tone entered the room.

Miriam’s assistant stepped closer to the stage.

Diane released the key. It rose slowly and stopped level with the others.

“Sticking key,” she said.

Evan looked toward the scenery flat. “And the scrape?”

Diane crossed to stage right. The flat rested on two casters, one wheel turned sideways. A fresh curved mark crossed the black-painted floor behind it. She pulled the frame two inches. The crooked caster dragged and produced a shorter version of the sound they had heard downstairs.

“Movement source available,” Abby said.

“Available,” Evan repeated. “No one was up here to move it.”

Diane returned the flat to the mark. “We have no continuous coverage between rehearsal and entry.”

Miriam stood near the center aisle with her eyes on the stage. She had left the ritual card in her pocket. Her hands remained open at her sides.

“The basement pressure is absent here,” she said.

Diane pressed her recorder marker. “What is present?”

Miriam walked to the front row and stopped beside the seat with the hat beneath it. “A woman. Older. She associates herself with performance, but not with the students in the way the young person downstairs seemed to.”

Evan raised his camera.

Diane said, “No questions that supply a period, occupation, or death.”

“I was going to ask where she stands.”

“Ask that.”

Evan faced Miriam. “Where is the woman?”

Miriam looked toward the stage-left wing. “Near the curtain. She prefers watching from the side.”

The work light cast the fly ropes as narrow vertical shadows on the wall. Dust marked the folds of the main curtain. A glass display case stood beside the wing door, half hidden by stacked music stands.

Miriam continued. “She cared about language. Timing. The exact way a line landed.”

“A director,” Evan said.

Abby marked the contribution.

“Perhaps,” Miriam replied. “Or a writer. Someone who corrected people because the work mattered to her.”

Diane walked to the display case. Old programs, cast photographs, and engraved plaques filled three shelves. The nearest program faced outward beneath the glass. Its cover read BELLWEATHER PLAYERS, WINTER SEASON. Beneath that, in smaller print: A HOUSE OF PAPER, directed by Marian Bell.

Diane angled her body between the case and Miriam.

“Hold,” she said.

Everyone stopped.

Abby brought her camera close to the glass. “Visible program. Name and theatrical role exposed from the center aisle at entry.”

Evan moved toward it. “Marian Bell.”

Miriam looked at him. “I had not offered a name.”

“You said writer or director.”

“After standing within view of the display.”

The assistant leaned around Diane. “Could she have read it without realizing?”

“Yes,” Miriam said.

Evan crouched beside the case. “Or the program could be here because the woman remains here.”

Diane pointed at his phone. “Keep it dark.”

“I want to check the school archive.”

“No search until Miriam finishes the impression.”

He held the phone but left the screen black.

Miriam faced the stage again. “The name sound carries M and R. Mary. Marion. Marian. Something close.”

Evan looked at Diane.

Abby marked the sequence aloud. “Visible Marian Bell program precedes M-R name approximation.”

Miriam nodded. “Record that clearly.”

She moved along the front row. “The woman presents as pleased by the room. She regards it as work still in progress rather than a place of confinement.”

“Does she want to leave?” the assistant asked.

Diane turned toward her.

The assistant lowered her eyes. “That supplied a choice.”

“It also matters,” Miriam said. “I can answer as an impression. She shows no wish to leave.”

Evan’s phone lit in his hand.

Diane crossed the aisle. “Search terms aloud.”

“Marian Bell theater.”

Abby repeated the terms and time.

Results filled the screen. Evan scrolled.

“Local obituary. Wedding announcement. Music teacher. Another Marian Bell in Oregon.”

“Record rejected matches,” Diane said.

Abby began listing them.

Evan added a word. “Playwright.”

The new results included library catalogs, a theater-history blog, scanned newspapers, and several people with similar names. He opened an image result showing a woman in a dark dress beside a stage curtain.

“Here.”

The page identified Marian Bell as a regional playwright whose comedy The Winter Guest had opened in 1927 at the Grand Vaudeville Theatre in another state. A small portrait sat above a paragraph about touring repertory companies. Nothing on the page connected Marian Bell to Bellweather High, its city, or the program in the display case.

Evan held the phone beside the glass.

“Same name. Theater. Vaudeville. Look at the seats in the old photograph.”

The article included an exterior photograph of the Grand Vaudeville Theatre and a later interior restoration. Rows of red seats curved toward a proscenium stage.

Diane looked across Bellweather’s auditorium. Its seats also carried red upholstery. Most school theaters built or renovated during the same decades used similar layouts, curtains, aisle lamps, and paint.

Abby said, “Search reached historical identity after name exposure and added occupation term. Venue keyword appears in result text.”

Miriam approached the phone but kept several feet between them. “The portrait resembles the impression only in age and posture.”

Evan enlarged the image. “You described a writer who corrects lines and watches from the wing.”

“I described an association with language, timing, and performance. We read a director named Marian Bell from the visible program. You added playwright and vaudeville through the search.”

“The convergence still exists.”

“So does the path.”

